From b9c441934402822c5c3a68810f3d356ce341401c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arena Agent <agent@arena.ai>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 03:15:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(buildcheck): detect the project type and plan bounded,
 classified checks

Fifth slice: knowing what a workspace is and whose fault a failure was, before
any build runs.

core/buildcheck (not core/build: .gitignore ignores build/, so a package with
that name is silently skipped by git add - see docs/BUILD_CHECKS.md):
- ProjectDetector works from the file list only (no execution, no SDK, no
  network), so it is unit testable and honest on a phone. Android Gradle is
  distinguished from plain Gradle by requiring both a settings file and an
  AndroidManifest; every profile carries its evidence and an `unavailable` list
  ("needs an Android SDK and a JDK") so the UI can say that before a build fails
  for a reason that looks unrelated.
- CheckCommand keeps executable and args separate and passes them as argv, never
  through a shell; isSafe re-checks the allowlist (./gradlew, npm, npx, python3,
  cargo, go), rejects shell metacharacters in args, and clamps the time and
  output budgets. The detector drops unsafe checks, and a test asserts that
  every generated check for every project type is safe.
- FailureClassifier orders timeout -> environment -> test -> code -> unknown.
  Environment first is the point: a missing SDK fills the log with `error:`
  lines, so the naive order would report every setup problem as broken code.
  Unknown stays unknown instead of being guessed; evidence is one trimmed
  200-char line, and the stored output is cut to the command's cap.

Deliberately not implemented, and said so in docs/BUILD_CHECKS.md: execution
(that goes through the existing runtime/terminal path with core/policy in front
of it, not a hidden ProcessBuilder in a core module), per-test report parsing,
and any retry loop - the classifier only reports whether a retry could help.

Tests: ProjectDetectorTest (12), FailureClassifierTest (9).
