+# Project Detection & Check Planning
+
+`core/buildcheck` answers two questions before any build happens: *what is this workspace*, and *which bounded
+checks may the app run on it* - plus, after a check fails, *whose fault was it*.
+
+## Why the package is called `buildcheck`
+
+The repository's `.gitignore` ignores `build/` (the Gradle output directory, and `docs/superpowers/` too). A
+package named `core/build` is therefore silently skipped by `git add .` - which is exactly what happened
+while this change was being prepared. Renaming it to `core/buildcheck` avoids a foot-gun that would have
+looked like "my new files did not commit" to the next contributor.
+
+## Files
+
+| File | Responsibility |
+| --- | --- |
+| `ProjectProfile.kt` | `ProjectType`, `CheckCategory`, `CheckCommand` (+ `isSafe`), `ProjectProfile`, `CheckResult`, `FailureClass` |
+| `ProjectDetector.kt` | filename-based detection and the per-type check list |
+| `FailureClassifier.kt` | output → `ENVIRONMENT` / `CODE` / `TEST` / `TIMEOUT` / `UNKNOWN` |
+
+Tests: `ProjectDetectorTest` (12), `FailureClassifierTest` (9).
+
+## Detection
+
+Filename-only, in this order, first match wins: Android Gradle (a `settings.gradle(.kts)` **and** an
+`AndroidManifest.xml`) → Gradle JVM → Node (`package.json`) → Python (`pyproject.toml`/`requirements.txt`/
+`setup.py`) → Rust (`Cargo.toml`) → Go (`go.mod`) → unknown.
+
+Every profile carries its `evidence` (the files that produced the verdict) and its `unavailable` list, so
+the UI can say "this needs an Android SDK and a JDK the device may not have" *before* a build fails for a
+reason that looks unrelated. Detection never reads file contents and never executes anything, so a project
+that hides its build files in an unusual layout will be reported as `UNKNOWN` - which is the honest answer,
+and the profile says "ask the user how this project is built" rather than guessing.
+
+## Check safety
+
+`CheckCommand` keeps the executable and the arguments separate and passes them as argv, never through a
+shell. `isSafe` enforces:
+
+- the executable is one of `./gradlew`, `gradlew`, `npm`, `npx`, `python3`, `python`, `cargo`, `go`;
+- no argument contains `;`, `|`, `&`, `$`, `` ` ``, `>`, `<`, or a newline;
+- the time budget is 1 ms–30 min and the output cap 1 B–4 MiB.
+
+`ProjectDetector` filters unsafe checks out entirely, and the test suite asserts that every generated check
+for every project type is safe - so a future edit that adds a clever `sh -c` line fails a test rather than
+shipping.
+
+## Failure classification
+
+Rules are checked in this order, deliberately:
+
+1. **timeout** - the only retryable class;
+2. **environment** - missing SDK/`ANDROID_HOME`, unresolvable dependencies, network, disk, permissions,
+   `command not found`;
+3. **test** - "There were failing tests", assertion failures;
+4. **code** - `Unresolved reference`, `error:`, ktlint/detekt/spotless, TypeScript errors;
+5. otherwise **unknown**, with no evidence invented.
+
+Environment before code is the point: a missing SDK prints `error:` lines throughout the log, so the naive
+ordering would report every setup problem as "your code is broken" and send the next task step in the wrong
+direction. `CheckResult.failureEvidence` is one trimmed line around the match, capped at 200 characters, and
+`output` itself is truncated to the command's `maxOutputBytes`.
+
+## What is not implemented
+
+Execution. `CheckCommand` describes a bounded command; running it requires the device's process facilities,
+which in this app means going through the existing runtime/terminal path with the policy engine in front of
+it (`core/policy`), not a new `ProcessBuilder` hidden inside a core module. That integration is the next
+change, and this document says so instead of implying a build runner exists.
+
+Also not implemented: parsing test reports into per-test results (only the class of failure is derived), and
+any retry loop - `FailureClassifier` reports whether a retry *could* help, `core/task` decides whether to
+spend one.
